WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.554
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.554 --> 00:00:09.984
When I was nine,
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:00:09.984 --> 00:00:14.251
&gt;&gt; My favorite pastime wasn't recess or

00:00:14.251 --> 00:00:17.458
video games or even sports.

00:00:17.458 --> 00:00:18.705
It was statistics.

00:00:18.705 --> 00:00:23.380
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That taught myself alone in my bedroom

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:26.990
sorting basketball cards
from best to worst,

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:28.720
using the numbers on
the back of the cards.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.950
It was back then, that I've learned a very
important lesson of stats 101, that

00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:40.046
I could make the numbers see
just about anything I wanted.

00:00:40.046 --> 00:00:43.230
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; My childhood idol

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:46.360
was a basketball player named
Anthony Penny Hardaway.

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:53.380
He played in an era with perhaps
the greatest of all time, Michael Jordan.

00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:59.650
But this introverted fourth grader
was determined to build a statistical

00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:04.475
model that proved that in fact Penny
Hardaway was the best player in the world.

00:01:04.475 --> 00:01:06.330
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Now,

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:11.110
there were several common statistics
on the back of a basketball card,

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:13.330
the most important of
which is points per game.

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:18.370
Several players, including Jordan,
averaged more points than Penny.

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:22.310
So I started with a different metric,
rebounds per game.

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:26.370
And I eliminated anyone who
averaged less than five rebounds.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:32.360
Now this narrow the pool but
some survivors,

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:36.260
including Jordan,
still average more points than Penny.

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:40.870
So I narrow it even further
with assists per game.

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:43.625
But Michael Jordan remained.

00:01:43.625 --> 00:01:47.160
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:51.430
now that year I knew that Michael Jordan
missed several games due to injury.

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:56.140
Penny Hardaway, on the other hand,
played in all 82 games.

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:00.935
Surely, I convinced myself health and
durability matter.

00:02:00.935 --> 00:02:04.310
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; For the best player in the world, and

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:06.858
I eliminated anyone missing
more than four games.

00:02:06.858 --> 00:02:12.400
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And so,

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:17.540
after games played rebounds, assists
Penny Hardaway finally led the pack.

00:02:19.270 --> 00:02:21.525
I call it, The Penny Algorithm.

00:02:21.525 --> 00:02:24.830
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It is how I prove that my

00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:29.500
favorite basketball player was indeed
the best basketball player in the world.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:32.465
Simple, indisputable statistics.

00:02:32.465 --> 00:02:36.990
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That 20 years later,

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:41.000
I can tell you that the logic that
the Penny Algorithm used is actually

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:45.620
quite similar to the logic that your spam
filter uses to keep your inbox clean.

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:51.090
In fact, many machine learning
algorithms transform our daily lives

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:52.060
using such logic.

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:57.540
But you probably shouldn't trust
the model built by nine year olds.

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:00.810
And more broadly, and
more importantly, as I'll show,

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:04.750
we should all have the healthy
distrust for models and algorithms.

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:11.230
Take Amazon's hiring algorithm
abandoned in 2017 for gender bias.

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:17.240
This algorithm learn the characteristics
of Amazon's best employees and

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:20.670
apply that pattern to hundreds
of thousands of job applicants.

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:27.790
The problem was that at the time, Amazon's
top employees were predominantly male.

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:30.560
So the algorithm learned to
discount female applicants.

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:35.490
Even when they've removed
gender from the model,

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:38.650
the algorithm learned to use proxies for
gender and

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:42.650
discounted applicants for
phrases such as women's swim team.

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:44.150
Or attendance at an all women's school.

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:49.440
Now many people hope the objectivity
of machine learning,

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:53.780
will counter things like
gender bias in the workplace.

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:54.990
But the truth is,

00:03:54.990 --> 00:03:59.602
even machine learning algorithms
can be vulnerable to human bias.

00:04:03.074 --> 00:04:07.777
On our mission to fight biasing algorithm
is Joy Boom Weenie, an African American,

00:04:07.777 --> 00:04:11.830
computer scientist and the creator
of the algorithmic Justice League.

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:17.030
As a graduate student using the same
facial recognition software

00:04:17.030 --> 00:04:18.148
that's in our phones today.

00:04:18.148 --> 00:04:23.010
She noticed that the algorithms
that worked on her Caucasian male

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:28.065
lab partner did not work on her
dark skin or dark features.

00:04:28.065 --> 00:04:32.632
The problem, was that the algorithm
that the model was trained

00:04:32.632 --> 00:04:36.177
on a dataset that lacked
meaningful diversity.

00:04:36.177 --> 00:04:41.794
And the result was that the same
algorithm that works 99.7%

00:04:41.794 --> 00:04:46.796
of the time on my face works
just 65% of the time on Joy.

00:04:49.743 --> 00:04:54.346
We all hope that the technology we create
will have benefits that can be shared by

00:04:54.346 --> 00:04:55.110
all people.

00:04:57.272 --> 00:05:01.397
But even the benefits of machine
learning are vulnerable to human bias.

00:05:04.994 --> 00:05:09.602
Last fall, consumers were outraged when
Apple Card offered better terms to

00:05:09.602 --> 00:05:12.900
male applicants than female applicants.

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:15.040
Sometimes 20 times better,

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:19.670
even before collecting relevant financial
data such as credit score or income.

00:05:21.140 --> 00:05:24.630
The Internet exploded with similar such
examples once this story surfaced.

00:05:28.173 --> 00:05:34.410
But the problem is that those
algorithms do exactly as they're told.

00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:38.540
They maximize revenue by optimizing for
things such as click through rate and

00:05:38.540 --> 00:05:39.780
engagement.

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:41.282
Both of which vary by gender.

00:05:43.052 --> 00:05:46.700
Oftentimes, the goal of our
model obscures its consequences.

00:05:48.230 --> 00:05:49.119
And the truth is,

00:05:49.119 --> 00:05:53.103
machine learning algorithms can have
significant unintended consequences.

00:05:55.831 --> 00:06:00.588
Now, this technology has transformative
potential to live billions out of poverty

00:06:00.588 --> 00:06:04.680
and to accelerate human progress,
like few technologies ever before.

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:09.140
Indeed, it's why I've spent most of
my time at Stanford studying it.

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:15.668
But unlocking this potential comes
with risks and trade offs, and

00:06:15.668 --> 00:06:18.075
knowing when to trust the model and

00:06:18.075 --> 00:06:23.235
when not to requires that we first
understand these risks and trade offs.

00:06:25.743 --> 00:06:30.050
But assuming you're convinced to have
a healthy distrust for algorithms,

00:06:30.050 --> 00:06:32.213
how then do we know when to trust them?

00:06:34.494 --> 00:06:36.500
Here are three questions to ask.

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:41.445
I call them Penny's Trustworthy Framework.

00:06:41.445 --> 00:06:45.170
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Number one, what is this model true of?

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:50.430
Every model just like every one of our
perspectives is built upon assumptions.

00:06:51.490 --> 00:06:54.180
Bias often lurks within these assumptions.

00:06:56.570 --> 00:06:58.790
Number two, who or what is missing?

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:03.160
Removing gender from an algorithm
does not prevent gender bias.

00:07:04.470 --> 00:07:08.000
In fact, it's often how bias is
found measured and mitigated.

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:13.720
In number three, what
are the consequences of this algorithm.

00:07:15.340 --> 00:07:19.334
Any one of us could find ourselves in
a position through our actions and

00:07:19.334 --> 00:07:22.328
decisions to prevent
the scaling of unwanted bias.

00:07:24.424 --> 00:07:26.090
Consider my own experience.

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:31.540
While I was in the United States Air Force
I built a model to predict how students

00:07:31.540 --> 00:07:35.759
would perform through a nine-month
flight training program in the F-15.

00:07:37.610 --> 00:07:41.130
This model allowed us to identify
struggling students early and

00:07:41.130 --> 00:07:42.280
invest in them appropriately.

00:07:44.770 --> 00:07:49.110
As flight commander, I mention one
such student that the model predicted

00:07:49.110 --> 00:07:52.070
with high certainty just would
not graduate the program.

00:07:54.590 --> 00:07:56.270
I knew this data better than anyone.

00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:00.520
I'd spent hundreds of hours
training this model myself.

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:05.140
But I also knew that
student is a human being.

00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:07.220
I knew he was a husband.

00:08:07.220 --> 00:08:09.400
I knew he's a brand new father.

00:08:10.640 --> 00:08:13.980
I knew that he struggled with
air sickness on every flight,

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:15.640
just as I had four years prior.

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:24.670
While the algorithm encourages us to give
up hope, deep down I'd empathy for him.

00:08:24.670 --> 00:08:26.690
Should I trust the model or not?

00:08:29.390 --> 00:08:32.380
To the rise of machine learning and
artificial intelligence,

00:08:32.380 --> 00:08:36.850
many of you will face the same question
of whether to trust the model or not.

00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:40.470
I decided not to.

00:08:40.470 --> 00:08:45.000
But what matters more than my decision,
is that in the future each of us

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:48.950
takes responsibility for this decision,
and ownership of the consequences.

00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:51.600
But before you decide,

00:08:53.300 --> 00:08:55.677
remember Penny's Trustworthy Framework,
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:08:55.677 --> 00:08:57.850
&gt;&gt; And that shows you three questions.

00:08:57.850 --> 00:09:01.620
What's this true of, what's missing and
what are the consequences?

00:09:02.780 --> 00:09:09.346
Because making better decisions with
algorithms starts with trusting them less.

00:09:09.346 --> 00:09:11.564
Thank you.

00:09:11.564 --> 00:09:12.579
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:12.579 --> 00:09:17.371
[MUSIC]

